{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>Let me explain what lake humidity actually does to your home, because most people underestimate it.</p>

<p>Lake Guntersville covers 69,000 acres. All that surface water evaporates constantly — not just in summer, year-round. The result is a microclimate that keeps relative humidity 15-20% higher than areas just 30 miles inland. On a day when Huntsville's at 65% humidity, Goose Pond might be at 80%.</p>

<p>Here's what that means for your gutters and the wood around them:</p>

<p><strong>Fascia boards stay wet longer.</strong> Wood that would dry out in a day elsewhere might stay damp for three or four days near the lake. Wet wood rots. And once rot starts behind a gutter, you can't see it until the gutter starts pulling away from the house. By then, you're replacing wood, not just resealing a joint.</p>

<p><strong>Seams corrode faster.</strong> The seals on sectional gutters are rated for certain conditions. Lake humidity exceeds those conditions. We see 10-year-old sectional gutters near Guntersville with seam failures that shouldn't happen for another decade. Seamless gutters eliminate most of those failure points.</p>

<p><strong>Mold grows where you can't see it.</strong> Behind gutters, under soffit panels, in the gap between fascia and sheathing. That black staining on your gutters? That's the surface mold you can see. The structural damage is happening behind it.</p>

<p>The vacation home problem makes everything worse. A full-time resident notices when something looks off. A vacation home might sit six months between visits. The gutter that started leaking in March has caused $3,000 worth of fascia damage by September — and nobody knew.</p>

<p>What works in Madison doesn't automatically work at the lake. Different environment, different approach.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>If your Scottsboro property sits empty for weeks or months, here's how to keep gutter problems from turning into expensive surprises.</p>

<h4>Before You Leave</h4>
<p><strong>Clear gutters completely.</strong> Any debris you leave behind will hold moisture against the gutter bottom. In lake humidity, that accelerates corrosion. A gutter that drains dry stays healthier.</p>

<p><strong>Check downspout connections.</strong> A disconnected downspout dumps water right at your foundation for however long you're gone. I've seen vacation homes where a $2 elbow falling off caused $8,000 in foundation moisture damage over one rainy spring.</p>

<p><strong>Walk the perimeter and look up.</strong> Five minutes with binoculars can spot problems before they have months to get worse. Look for: gutter sag (more than 1/4" per 10 feet is a problem), staining below seams, any gap between gutter and fascia.</p>

<h4>Gutter Guards: Worth It for Vacation Homes</h4>
<p>For a house you live in, gutter guards are a convenience. For a house that sits empty, they're risk management.</p>

<p>Without guards, debris accumulates while you're away. That debris holds moisture. Moisture causes corrosion and rot. By the time you're back, damage has been compounding for months.</p>

<p>Not all guards work equally well in humid environments. Foam guards are a disaster near the lake — they absorb moisture and never dry out. Solid surface tension covers work okay but can get overwhelmed in heavy storms. Micro-mesh is usually the best choice: debris sheds, water flows, and there's no moisture-trapping foam to worry about.</p>

<h4>The Inspection Schedule That Works</h4>
<p><strong>Spring visit:</strong> Check for winter damage. Scottsboro doesn't freeze hard, but we get enough ice to stress gutters. Clear any debris, check hangers (they should be every 24 inches max — closer to 18 is better).</p>

<p><strong>Fall visit:</strong> This is the critical one. Clear leaves before winter rains. Debris that sits over winter turns into gutter concrete by spring — compacted, wet, heavy, and damaging.</p>

<p><strong>Can't make it regularly?</strong> Schedule professional cleanings for late November and mid-April. We handle dozens of <a href="/recent-projects/">vacation properties</a> this way — owners don't have to remember to call, we just show up on schedule.</p>

<h4>Communication When You're Away</h4>
<p>Give us a contact method that works for you (text, email, phone), let us know if there's a keycode or neighbor who can provide access, and we'll document everything with photos. You'll see what we saw, what we did, and what needs attention — even if you won't be back for months.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set local_spotlight %}
<p>Scottsboro isn't one market — it's several, each with different gutter challenges.</p>

<h4>Goose Pond Colony and the Lakefront</h4>
<p><a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond</a> properties are the textbook lake humidity problem. Beautiful views, constant moisture stress. Most of these homes were built in the 80s and 90s, which means they're hitting the age where original gutters fail and deferred maintenance catches up.</p>

<p>The slopes are tricky too. Lakefront lots pitch toward the water — which is great for views but means water runs back toward lower-level living spaces if gutters don't catch it properly. Underground drains are common here, routing water away from foundations and downhill toward the lake.</p>

<p>Vacation ownership is heavy in Goose Pond. We do a lot of inspection and documentation work here — owners want to know what's happening to their property between visits.</p>

<h4>Downtown Scottsboro and the Historic Core</h4>
<p>The houses around the courthouse square and along <a href="/recent-projects/">South Broad Street</a> are some of the oldest in Jackson County. 1920s craftsmans, 1950s ranches, a few Victorian-era holdouts. These homes have character — and complicated rooflines that stock gutters can't handle.</p>

<p>We do a lot of custom work downtown: half-round gutters to match historic profiles, copper accents on preserved homes, sectional replacements that follow every jog and bay window in the roofline. The work takes longer but it's necessary to do it right.</p>

<p>The drainage problem downtown is different from the lake. Flat lots, old storm sewers that back up in heavy rain, and clay soil that doesn't absorb water. Downspout extensions need to go further — 6 feet minimum, 10 is better.</p>

<h4>Highway 72 Corridor and Newer Development</h4>
<p>The growth along 72 toward Huntsville has brought newer construction to Scottsboro — 2000s and 2010s subdivisions with builder-grade gutters. Same problems we see in Madison: undersized 5-inch gutters with 2x3 downspouts, trying to handle roof areas that need 6-inch with 3x4.</p>

<p>If you're in a newer development and your gutters overflow every storm, they're probably undersized. The builder saved $200 going with smaller gutters. You're living with the consequences.</p>
{% endset %}
